WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.837
BIND does three jobs, and NIST and
ISC both say to keep them apart.

2
00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:10.200
NIST says an internet-facing server shouldn't
serve in both roles, and ISC agrees.

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.338
In a cloud, the forwarder
is the common case.

4
00:00:13.338 --> 00:00:17.226
It holds no zones; it applies
agency policy and logging,

5
00:00:17.226 --> 00:00:20.250
then forwards each name
to the right place.

6
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.491
A recursive resolver fits where agency
policy and logging must apply at the first

7
00:00:25.491 --> 00:00:30.500
hop, with the same response policy zones,
validation and logging as on premises.

8
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:35.402
And authoritative service fits only for
a delegated zone the native services

9
00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:39.550
can't answer, or as a secondary of
a zone mastered on the DDI platform.

10
00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.900
Never a separately authored primary.
